Genetic Analysis of SCA 2 and 3
Repeat Expansions in Essential
Tremor and Atypical
Parkinsonism
Eng-King Tan, MD, 1,2* Justina Tong, BSc, 3
Ratnagopal Pavanni, MD, 1 Meng-Cheong Wong, MD, 1
and Yi Zhao, MD 3
1Department of Neurology, Singapore General Hospital,
Singapore; 2National Neuroscience Institute,
Singapore;3Department of Clinical Research, Singapore
General Hospital, Singapore
Abstract: Anecdotal reports suggest that patients with
spinocerebellar ataxia (SCA 2) patients can present with
postural tremor with ataxia. We determined the prevalence
of SCA2 and SCA3 mutations in a cohort of ET and atyp-
ical Parkinsonism patients. A total of 277 subjects compris-
ing of 177 ET and 100 atypical Parkinsonism were exam-
ined. We identiﬁed one positive case of SCA3 among those
who were diagnosed with ET, yielding a prevalence of
0.5%, but a zero prevalence among our atypical Parkinson-
ism patients. No study subjects carried an abnormal SCA2
repeat expansion. Our study highlights that SCA3 can
present initially with ET symptoms, expanding the spec-
trum of genetic diseases that can be associated with ET-like
phenotype. Routine screening for SCA2 and SCA3 in ET
and atypical Parkinsonism patients may not be cost effec-
tive. However, in the long-term follow-up of patients who
present with an ET phenotype, clinicians should be vigilant
for other neurological signs, which may be point to an
alternate diagnosis. © 2007 Movement Disorder Society
Key words: SCA2; SCA3; Parkinsonism; essential tremor.
Spinocerebellar ataxias (SCAs) refer to a group of
neurodegenerative diseases characterized by cerebellar
dysfunction alone or in combination with other neuro-
logical abnormalities.
1,2 It is well recognized that some
SCA2 and SCA3 patients may present with levodopa
responsive parkinsonism, with or without ataxia, and
other neurological signs.
1-3 Limited literature suggests
that the prevalence of SCA2 in patients with familial
levodopa responsive parkinsonism is estimated to be
1– 8%, most commonly observed in Chinese.
4
Case reports have highlighted that SCA 2 patients can
present with levodopa resistant parkinsonism, and prom-
inent postural tremor with ataxia.
4 Although these anec-
dotal reports are interesting, the practical issue regarding
the clinical utility of a routine screen for SCA2 and
SCA3 in subjects presenting with a phenotype resem-
bling typical essential tremor (ET) and levodopa resistant
parkinsonism has rarely been investigated. Such infor-
mation would help guide clinical decision for SCA test-
ing in the frequently encountered “common garden va-
riety” of ET and atypical parkinsonian disorders. To
address this, we conducted a study to determine the
prevalence of SCA2 and SCA3 mutations in a cohort of
ET and atypical parkinsonism patients.
